      "hint": "Use $\\frac{1}{\\alpha} + \\frac{1}{\\beta} = \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{\\alpha\\beta} = 4$ to relate $q$ and $r$. Combine with the A.P. condition $2q = p + r$ to express all coefficients in terms of $r$.",
      "explanation": "Given $\\frac{1}{\\alpha} + \\frac{1}{\\beta} = 4 \\implies \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{\\alpha\\beta} = 4$.\n\nFrom the equation $px^2 + qx + r = 0$:\n$$\\alpha + \\beta = -\\frac{q}{p}, \\quad \\alpha\\beta = \\frac{r}{p}$$\n$$\\implies -\\frac{q}{r} = 4 \\implies q = -4r$$\n\nSince $p, q, r$ are in A.P.:\n$$2q = p + r$$\n$$2(-4r) = p + r \\implies -8r = p + r \\implies p = -9r$$\n\nNow, calculate $|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\frac{\\sqrt{D}}{|p|} = \\frac{\\sqrt{q^2 - 4pr}}{|p|}$:\n$$|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\frac{\\sqrt{(-4r)^2 - 4(-9r)(r)}}{|-9r|} = \\frac{\\sqrt{16r^2 + 36r^2}}{9|r|}$$\n$$|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\frac{\\sqrt{52r^2}}{9|r|} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}|r|}{9|r|} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{9}$$",
      "media": null
    },
    {
