        "$(\\alpha - \\beta)(\\gamma - \\delta)(\\alpha + \\beta)(\\gamma + \\delta)$"
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "$(\\alpha - \\gamma)(\\beta - \\gamma)(\\alpha + \\delta)(\\beta + \\delta)$",
      "positiveMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 1,
      "hint": "Write $q^2 - p^2 = (q - p)(q + p)$ and substitute $p = -(\\alpha + \\beta)$ and $q = -(\\gamma + \\delta)$. Expand and factorize into linear terms involving $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta$.",
      "explanation": "From the given equations:\n$$\\alpha + \\beta = -p, \\quad \\alpha\\beta = 1$$\n$$\\gamma + \\delta = -q, \\quad \\gamma\\delta = 1$$\n\nNow, calculate $q^2 - p^2$:\n$$q^2 - p^2 = (\\gamma + \\delta)^2 - (\\alpha + \\beta)^2 = (\\gamma + \\delta - \\alpha - \\beta)(\\gamma + \\delta + \\alpha + \\beta)$$\n\nUsing algebraic identity expansion, this factors into:\n$$q^2 - p^2 = (\\alpha - \\gamma)(\\beta - \\gamma)(\\alpha + \\delta)(\\beta + \\delta)$$",
